  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Trump-Xi Beijing summit (May 14–15) produced a White House statement that the Strait of Hormuz "must remain open" and that Iran "can never have a nuclear weapon," with Xi stating publicly that China opposes Hormuz militarization and offering to help broker Iran peace. The Chinese government's own readout, however, omitted any mention of Iran, and Xi simultaneously reaffirmed continued Chinese oil purchases from Tehran, limiting the operational weight of the joint language. Transit data as of May 14 shows only six vessels crossing Hormuz per day against a 130-vessel pre-crisis baseline, and Lloyd's war-risk premiums remain at approximately 2.5% of hull value per transit — well above the 1.5% indicator trigger. No Western shipping line has announced a return to scheduled Hormuz transit. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>The Israel-Lebanon ceasefire, due to expire EOD May 18, was the subject of a third round of US-mediated Washington talks on May 14–15. Lebanon's delegation demanded a permanent ceasefire and full Israeli withdrawal timeline; Israel's delegation maintained its requirement for freedom of military operations until Hezbollah's military capacity is resolved. No tier-1 source reported a breakthrough; multiple outlets described "cautious optimism" for an extension without confirmed progress on the structural gap. The last confirmed IDF airstrike in Beirut governorate (Dahiyeh/Haret Hreik) occurred May 6. The May 13 Jiyeh drone strikes — killing twelve including two children — occurred on the coastal highway approximately 20km south of Beirut in Mount Lebanon governorate, confirmed by AP, Reuters, and the Lebanese Health Ministry. No Beirut-governorate strike was reported on May 14 or May 15 by any qualifying source, closing F4 as NO. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>; moved <span class="cite">N2</span></p>

      <p>Iranian Foreign Minister Araghchi traveled to BRICS New Delhi on May 13–14, where he urged member nations to condemn "US-Israeli unlawful aggression" and accused the UAE of direct military involvement in operations against Iran. Iran's Foreign Ministry spokesman reiterated on May 12 that war must end and Hormuz must be unblocked before nuclear discussions can begin — a sequencing-first framing that is functionally incompatible with the US MOU's structure, though it uses the word "prerequisite" rather than "rejection." VP Vance stated May 13 that "progress is being made" while acknowledging the nuclear red line has not been bridged, maintaining negotiations as nominally open without advancing the framework document. The enrichment moratorium gap (US: approximately 20 years; Iran: 5 years) remained unbridged with three days to the May 18 horizon. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move; moved <span class="cite">F3</span> modestly</p>

      <p>Saudi Arabia and Kuwait lifted restrictions on US military basing and overflight access at Prince Sultan Air Base around May 8, restoring operational conditions for Project Freedom — a development reported by WSJ citing unnamed US officials, corroborated by Defense News. F5's resolution criteria require a named DoD source in Reuters, Bloomberg, or Times of Israel, or a Saudi MOFA statement; no such public confirmation has been issued in the seven days since operational restoration, consistent with Saudi practice of quiet operational cooperation without formal public announcement. Saudi domestic-audience sensitivity to publicly hosting US military operations against a Muslim-majority neighboring state constitutes a structural disincentive to public confirmation that is assessed as the dominant factor. Ten days remain on the horizon. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset dissolution bill, co-sponsored by all four coalition factions (Likud, Shas, UTJ including Degel HaTorah, Religious Zionism/Otzma Yehudit), cleared the submission stage, and Times of Israel confirmed May 15 that the earliest first-reading vote can occur is Wednesday May 20 per Knesset procedural rules. The co-sponsorship of all factions fulfills N1's primary indicator condition. The opposition submitted parallel dissolution bills from Yesh Atid, Yisrael Beytenu, Democrats, and Ra'am, reinforcing procedural pressure on Speaker Ohana to schedule the vote within the May 20–28 window. Netanyahu's coalition has not lost its Knesset majority by defection — the government is dissolving itself by consent, not collapsing through defection, a distinction tracked separately under F7. → moved <span class="cite">N1</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

