      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (58%):</strong> Dominant scenario: F2 (memo signed, 7%) and F6 (Hormuz reopens, 9%) are near-floor, F3 (formal rejection, 22%) is below 25%, and N2 (Lebanon ceasefire extended, 68%) reflects extension without resolution — all consistent with a prolonged stalemate in which the Iran-US channel stays nominally open, Lebanon's ceasefire is repeatedly short-extended, and Hormuz remains commercially closed.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (14%):</strong> Requires at minimum F2 not resolving NO on May 18 (or a successor deal emerging in the F6 window) AND F6 moving toward the 50-vessel threshold; N2-YES (Lebanon ceasefire extended) is a necessary but not sufficient condition, and F6 at 9% sets an approximate upper bound for full de-escalation.</p>
    </div>
