  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's state media posture over the past 48 hours fits the pattern of a government managing two contradictory messages simultaneously. Press TV ran Araghchi's BRICS Delhi statement verbatim — "Iran urges BRICS nations to condemn US-Israeli aggression" — while simultaneously running Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmail Baghaei's formulation that Hormuz unblocking and war cessation are "prerequisites" to any nuclear discussion. This sequencing-first language is Iran's formal public position, but it is carefully calibrated to avoid the word "rejection": Baghaei used "prerequisite" rather than "refusal," leaving rhetorical room for a channel to remain open. IRNA covered Araghchi's Beijing Wang Yi talks favorably, emphasizing China's affirmation of Iran's "right to peaceful nuclear energy" — language that publicly validates Tehran's red line on enrichment rights without closing the door to a moratorium arrangement. Tasnim and Fars News gave prominent play to Trump's dismissal of Iran's counter-proposal as a "piece of garbage," framing it as proof of US bad faith rather than Iranian intransigence. The Kayhan editorial line this week called Iran's counter-proposal "reasonable and generous" while describing US demands as "complete surrender" — rejection-grade rhetoric at the hardliner outlet, but below the threshold of a Supreme Leader family statement or a named Araghchi declaration against the specific US-authored document. The net effect: Iran's state media apparatus is holding the line on pre-conditions while preserving a face-saving diplomatic off-ramp, with no formal MOU rejection confirmed in tier-1 sources. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without exceeding resolution threshold; supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

